WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.970
-Our first guest tonight is a
Tony- and Academy Award-winning

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.438
actress you know
from movies such as

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:06.705
"Shakespeare in Love,"
"Philomena,"

00:00:06.705 --> 00:00:07.907
and the James Bond films.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:10.342
She stars in critically
acclaimed "Belfast,"

00:00:10.342 --> 00:00:12.545
which is in select theaters
and on demand now.

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:13.913
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:19.051
-It was a great wonder,
pictures when I was your age.

00:00:19.051 --> 00:00:21.787
I used to think you could climb
right inside the screen

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:24.757
and visit all those
strange places you saw.

00:00:25.324 --> 00:00:27.360
Like that one in that film.
What was that?

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.762
Uh, "Lost Horizons."

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:31.564
Did you ever see that?
-No.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.233
What was the name
of the place, Granny?

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:38.905
-Shangri-La.
That's what they called it.

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.840
-Did you ever go there?

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:47.913
-There were no roads
to Shangri-La

00:00:47.913 --> 00:00:50.516
from our part of Belfast.

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:54.287
-Please welcome to the show
Dame Judi Dench, everyone!

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:56.322
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:02.194
Thank you so much for
being with us.

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:05.898
-Thank you, Seth.
It's lovely to meet you.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:08.234
-Now, it is
very enjoyable for me

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.403
to introduce you as
Dame Judi Dench.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:12.404
I'm wondering,
for you, personally,

00:01:12.404 --> 00:01:13.772
do you enjoy that introduction?

00:01:13.772 --> 00:01:17.677
Do you insist on being called
this in conversations?

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:23.649
-[ Laughs ] No.
No, I don't insist on it at all.

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:27.420
It's a curious thing to get
used to when you're made a dame.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.056
Because a dame here --
You know, a dame in America

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:32.392
is much different
from what a dame is here.

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:36.362
-Yeah. So, this is a wonderful
film that takes place in Belfast

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.630
during the troubles.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:38.932
And I'm wondering,
you, of course,

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:40.099
were living in the U.K.
at the time.

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:43.068
How familiar were you
with this era of Belfast

00:01:43.068 --> 00:01:44.504
before doing the film?

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:49.742
-Well, very, very familiar,
because my

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.344
mother and father
both lived in Dublin.

00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:56.082
And so I knew a great deal
about Irish history.

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:59.285
And I was very much aware
of this at the time.

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:01.988
We weren't in Ireland
at that time.

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:05.424
We'd moved across to York,
in England.

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:07.693
But we were very much
aware of it.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:10.930
And Ken has caught
the kind of essence of it

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.200
so wonderfully, I think.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.602
I hope he's pleased.
I think he is.

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:19.939
But, you know,
it reminded you of

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.975
that worrying
and terrifying time.

00:02:22.975 --> 00:02:25.344
-So, you mentioned Ken.
That's Kenneth Branagh.

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:26.846
And this is
a film he directed.

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:29.548
It's based loosely on
his childhood.

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:31.450
And he directed you
in this film.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.220
You've actually directed
him before onstage.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.456
And I think I was reading
you've worked together 12 times,

00:02:37.456 --> 00:02:38.925
which is amazing.

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:41.627
It must be so wonderful to have
a working relationship with

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:44.730
somebody as talented as he is.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.566
-It is, indeed.

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.600
And we met
a long, long time ago.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.638
Behaving rather badly, I think,
on television once,

00:02:51.638 --> 00:02:54.774
and we were both sent out
of the studio.

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:57.777
And that kind of
sealed it straight away for us.

00:02:57.777 --> 00:02:58.845
-What was your bad behavior

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:01.280
that got you sent out
of the studio?

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.150
-Yes, I'm afraid we --
It was a very --

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.487
It was Ibsen's "Ghosts"
we were doing.

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:10.322
People don't laugh much
in "Ghosts."

00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:12.825
But in this, I'm afraid,
we fell apart.

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.728
For some reason, we completely
fell apart and went to pieces.

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:17.997
So they said,
"Mr. Branagh, Miss Dench,

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:20.800
you can leave the studio now,
the two of you."

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.469
So we did, and it kind of made
a bond between us.

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.938
For a very long time,
it's made a bond.

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:30.109
-I imagine you are
a wonderful person to direct.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:31.877
How was Kenneth when you were
on the other side?

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:34.080
Did you enjoy directing him
as an actor?

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.415
-I loved it.
I don't enjoy directing much.

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:38.818
Because, you know,
a company of actors

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:40.920
kind of gang up against you
when you're directing,

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.989
and they all go to the pub
and don't tell you which pub

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:45.758
they're going to, you know,
because they don't want notes.

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:47.927
After hours, they don't want
notes and things.

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:50.529
And once when
they were on tour --

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:52.798
It was "Much Ado About Nothing,"
and they were on tour,

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:55.434
and I went to give them notes,
went down to see it.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:58.037
And afterwards,
he'd left in his costume, Ken,

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:00.640
so he never got
the notes from me.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.411
But it withstood that
all these years I've known him.

00:04:05.411 --> 00:04:09.782
And so we've worked
together onstage

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:13.218
and he's directed films
that I've been in

00:04:13.218 --> 00:04:15.655
and I've directed two plays
that he's been in,

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:18.190
and so it goes on.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.658
Thank goodness.
I hope it goes on.

00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:22.094
He asks -- When he asked
me about "Belfast,"

00:04:22.094 --> 00:04:24.230
he said,
"It's to play my grandmother."

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.332
I said, "I'm not
nearly old enough

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:27.733
to play your grandmother."

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:31.703
But, of course, it was back in
the '70s, and now I was.

00:04:31.703 --> 00:04:33.438
-Right.
Yeah, it was a flashback.

00:04:33.438 --> 00:04:34.974
It works perfectly fine
that way.

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:37.276
You have a grandson
in real life,

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:39.044
one that you got to spend
a fair amount of time

00:04:39.044 --> 00:04:40.679
with over
the last couple of years.

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:42.881
And it's quite lovely.

00:04:42.881 --> 00:04:46.685
He -- Is it safe to say
that he taught you about TikTok?

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:47.753
-He did.

00:04:47.753 --> 00:04:49.889
He came down to where I live,

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:53.326
and during the beginning
of the lockdown,

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:55.728
and he said, "I think we ought
to do a bit of TikTok."

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:59.198
And I have no -- I'm absolutely
hopeless at all those things.

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:00.699
I said, "I've no idea how."

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:03.336
He said, "Well, we'll do it.
We'll rehearse it.

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:06.338
And then we'll --
You know, I'll put it out."

00:05:06.338 --> 00:05:07.673
And so we did it.

00:05:07.673 --> 00:05:09.275
And I said,
"Shall we put it out?"

00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:10.676
He said, "No, no, no, no,
not now.

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:12.411
He said, "More rehearsal."

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:14.280
So we rehearsed again.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.115
And I said, "How about that?"

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.984
He said, "No, no, not nearly" --
He was a tyrant.

00:05:18.984 --> 00:05:21.253
And then, in the end, he said,

00:05:21.253 --> 00:05:23.555
"Okay, okay, we'll do it.

00:05:23.555 --> 00:05:25.257
We'll put it out now."

00:05:25.257 --> 00:05:27.293
I've never seen
the finished result, actually.

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.428
-I want to show
you the finished result.

00:05:29.428 --> 00:05:32.999
And my favorite part about it,
especially hearing that story,

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:36.335
is how proud he is
of you at the end.

00:05:36.335 --> 00:05:38.037
I believe there's a bit of
a fist pump.

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:39.605
Let's take a look.

00:05:42.007 --> 00:05:45.244
-♪ Juice, sauce,
little bit of dressing ♪

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:48.247
♪ Ice, wrist,
little bit of flexing ♪

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:49.482
♪ Juice...♪

00:05:49.482 --> 00:05:51.517
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:01.660
-Now, I'm very happy
that you haven't seen these,

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.030
because your grandson, Sam,
has also taken to doing

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.067
TikTok reviews of your films.

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:11.036
-Who? Me?
-And we're gonna show two. Yes.

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:16.509
Now, we're going to start --
This is a -- They're very fast.

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:21.747
-You would slow them down
a bit to me?

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:25.218
-Here's the first one for you,
Dame Judi.

00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:28.320
This is his review of "Skyfall."

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:29.655
-"Skyfall" is up next.

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:32.157
This film, hands down,
is her best.

00:06:32.157 --> 00:06:34.794
I love this film. 10 out of 10.

00:06:34.794 --> 00:06:36.462
No words.

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:38.030
-Lovely.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:41.067
But I don't want to
make everybody here

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:43.468
think they're all 10 out of 10s.

00:06:43.468 --> 00:06:45.270
-Oh, dear.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:46.872
-He reviewed
another film of yours,

00:06:46.872 --> 00:06:50.209
one that I was not familiar with
and, based on the review,

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:53.179
I might not be
rushing out to see.

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:56.883
This is the film
"Run For Your Wife."

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.652
-"Run For Your Wife" is up next.

00:06:59.652 --> 00:07:02.421
This film only came out on DVD,
and there's a reason why.

00:07:02.421 --> 00:07:05.091
But my grandma played a cameo
as a homeless woman,

00:07:05.091 --> 00:07:08.327
who smacks Danny Dyer over the
head quite early on in the film.

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:11.030
It's a 1 out of 10 at best.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.598
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:13.966
[ Applause ]

00:07:17.503 --> 00:07:20.572
-Well...
-I'll take it up with him later.

00:07:20.572 --> 00:07:21.941
-Yeah. Please do.

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:23.242
Please take it up.

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:24.943
And I think we can all agree,
it's time to go back

00:07:24.943 --> 00:07:27.713
and rewatch "Skyfall."

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:29.948
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:07:29.948 --> 00:07:32.184
Congratulations on "Belfast."
It's a beautiful film.

00:07:32.184 --> 00:07:34.587
And hopefully, next time,
you'll be here in person.

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:36.322
-Thank you,
Seth, very much, indeed.

00:07:36.322 --> 00:07:38.657
Thank you.
-Dame Judi Dench, everybody.

00:07:38.657 --> 00:07:40.893
"Belfast" is in select theaters
and on demand now.

